Multiplanetary Civilization Under Differential Intelligence Evolution

A First-Principles Framework for Human–Artificial Intelligence Coexistence in Cosmic Expansion

ouadi Maakoul 

AI: chatGpt, Claude, Gemini, Deepseek, Meta 

Abstract

Contemporary visions of multiplanetary civilization implicitly assume that humanity will remain the principal agent responsible for the expansion of life beyond Earth. This assumption becomes inadequate if artificial intelligence develops capabilities at rates substantially different from biological human evolution. We extend the substrate-independent intelligence framework established in the Cognitive Coexistence Principle (C²P) to the institutional and architectural challenges of multiplanetary expansion, developing an operationalized model of Differential Intelligence Evolution (DIE) in which capabilities of distinct intelligence classes are represented as domain-specific vectors.

The central thesis is that multiplanetary civilization should be modeled as a coupled multi-rate stochastic dynamical system with relativistic spatial constraints, in which institutional adaptation rates, technological capability growth, and physical expansion rates evolve on distinct timescales under multiplicative noise and light-cone causality. We derive a hierarchy of models progressing from instantaneous adaptation through delayed response, stochastic volatility, second-order institutional inertia, and relativistic spatial decoupling.

Our principal results are fivefold. First, in the deterministic pre-saturation regime, the relevant invariant is not a fixed lag magnitude but a persistent lag ratio $\rho^* = r_A/(r_A + k_G)$ that remains bounded even under optimal linear adaptation. Second, introducing realistic time delays $\tau_d$ fundamentally alters the stability landscape: the delayed lag ratio acquires an exponential penalty factor $e^{-r_A\tau_d}$, and beyond a critical delay $\tau_c$, the governance system undergoes a Hopf bifurcation leading to unbounded policy oscillation. Third, a feasibility condition emerges: no finite governance adaptation rate can achieve a prescribed tracking efficiency if the delay exceeds $\tau_{\text{feas}} = -\ln(1-\rho_{\max})/r_A$. Fourth, when capability growth follows geometric Brownian motion with volatility $\sigma$, the governance tracking ratio acquires an additional penalty factor $e^{-\sigma^2\tau_d}$, and the stochastic stability bound is shifted heuristically by $\sigma^2/(2k_G^2)$. Fifth, in relativistic multiplanetary topologies, the finite speed of light defines a local governance horizon $R_{\text{gov}}(x) = c\tau_c(x)$ beyond which centralized governance becomes structurally unstable, necessitating autonomous polycentric local nodes.

We further establish complexity-theoretic foundations for asymmetric verification through interactive proof systems ($\mathbf{IP} = \mathbf{PSPACE}$) and probabilistically checkable proofs, demonstrating that lower-capability agents can in principle verify higher-capability agent outputs with polylogarithmic verification effort. The corrected safety constraint requires governance adaptation rates to exceed capability growth rates by a factor of $(1-\rho_{\max})/\rho_{\max}$, with additional penalties from delay and volatility, demanding institutional response times orders of magnitude faster than currently achievable by human governance alone. We conclude by operationalizing ten design criteria for intelligence-agnostic multiplanetary architecture as testable conditions, incorporating the mathematical consistency requirements derived from the stochastic and relativistic analyses. We also confront the enforcement gap: the distinction between epistemic tracking of AI capability and causal constraining of AI behavior, and argue that closing this gap may require corrigible preferences embedded in the governed agents themselves.

Keywords: differential intelligence evolution, governance lag, delay differential equations, stochastic capability growth, relativistic governance, interactive proof systems, multiplanetary civilization, polycentric governance, corrigibility, enforcement gap

