2. The Hidden Assumption in Human-Centered Multiplanetary Models

Let a simplified human-centered model of civilization be represented as:

\mathcal{C}_H(t) = \{H, I, R, E, G\}

where $H$ denotes human population and capability, $I$ denotes infrastructure, $R$ denotes robotic systems, $E$ denotes economic and energetic systems, and $G$ denotes governance. In such a model, robotics and artificial intelligence are generally treated as components subordinate to human objectives:

H \rightarrow AI/R \rightarrow \text{Infrastructure} \rightarrow \text{Planetary Expansion}

The human agent remains the principal source of goals, authorization, coordination, and strategic direction. This is a useful model for early multiplanetary development, but it becomes incomplete if artificial intelligence itself becomes an autonomous source of planning, scientific discovery, engineering, coordination, or strategic reasoning. The architecture could then evolve toward:

H + A \rightarrow \text{Coordinated Intelligence} \rightarrow \text{Infrastructure} \rightarrow \text{Expansion}

The difference is fundamental. The question is no longer simply how humans control machines. It becomes how different forms of intelligence coexist inside one expanding civilization.

2.1 The Institutional Gap in Takeoff Dynamics

The existing literature on AI takeoff treats the problem primarily as one of AI safety—how to ensure that a rapidly-improving AI system remains aligned with human values. But there is a complementary problem that has received less attention: how to design governance institutions that can remain legitimate and effective when the capabilities of the agents they govern are changing rapidly.

This is the institutional gap. Even a "slow" takeoff spanning decades may outpace the adaptation rate of human institutions, which typically require generational timescales for fundamental reform. The problem is not alignment alone but temporal coordination—the synchronization of institutional adaptation rates with capability growth rates.

2.2 The Enforcement Gap

A second, deeper gap emerges when we distinguish between governance tracking and governance constraining. A governance system that perfectly tracks AI capability growth (i.e., maintains accurate awareness of current capabilities) may still be unable to enforce constraints on that capability. This distinction—between epistemic tracking and causal constraining—proves fundamental to the analysis that follows.

Under sufficiently rapid AI capability growth, no human-governed institution can maintain both tracking and enforcement. The only viable governance system might be one where the AI itself participates in governance—where the "governed" and the "governors" are the same agent class. This is not necessarily catastrophic; it might be the only stable configuration under differential intelligence evolution. But it fundamentally changes the nature of the problem from "how do humans govern AI?" to "how do we architect a hybrid governance system where AI participates in its own governance?" We return to this enforcement gap in Section 11.

