7. Second-Order Institutional Inertia

When governance processes exhibit internal inertia (bureaucracy, legislative debate, policy deployment lag), we model tracking as a second-order system driven by delayed input:

\frac{d^2 G(t)}{dt^2} + 2\zeta \omega_n \frac{dG(t)}{dt} + \omega_n^2 G(t) = \omega_n^2 A(t - \tau_d)

In the frequency domain ($s = i\omega$), the open-loop transfer function phase is:

\phi(\omega) = -\arctan\left( \frac{2\zeta \omega_n \omega}{\omega_n^2 - \omega^2} \right) - \omega \tau_d

The time delay reduces the system's Phase Margin (PM) linearly:

\text{PM}_{\text{delayed}} = \text{PM}_0 - \omega_c \tau_d

where $\omega_c$ is the gain crossover frequency. Overshoot magnitude $M_p$ escalates exponentially as $\text{PM}_{\text{delayed}} \to 0$:

M_p = \exp\left( -\frac{\pi \zeta_{\text{eff}}}{\sqrt{1 - \zeta_{\text{eff}}^2}} \right)

where $\zeta_{\text{eff}} \approx \frac{\text{PM}_{\text{delayed}}}{100^{\circ}}$.

Proposition 7 (Inertia-Delay Coupling). Any non-zero delay $\tau_d$ directly degrades damping, amplifying regulatory overshoot during capability acceleration. Second-order institutional inertia compounds the delay effect: even when $\tau_d < \tau_c$, the combination of inertia and delay reduces phase margin, producing oscillatory governance responses that may destabilize the system even before the formal Hopf threshold is reached.

Implication for Institutional Design. Human governance institutions typically exhibit both substantial inertia ($\zeta \ll 1$, underdamped) and significant delays ($\tau_d \sim \text{months to years}$). The combination is particularly dangerous: underdamped systems with delay exhibit "ringing"—repeated overcorrection cycles—which in the context of AI governance would manifest as cycles of under-regulation followed by over-regulation. This suggests that institutional design for differential intelligence evolution must prioritize both reducing delay (through predictive state estimation and early warning protocols) and increasing damping (through institutional structures that resist overcorrection).

