13. Positioning Relative to Existing Literature

13.1 Relation to Intelligence Explosion Literature

The recursive self-improvement loop discussed throughout this paper is a direct application of Good's (1965) intelligence explosion argument to the institutional domain. Where Good and his successors (Bostrom, Yudkowsky, Christiano) have focused on the safety problem—how to ensure that a recursively-improving AI remains aligned with human values—we focus on the institutional problem: how to design governance that remains legitimate when the capabilities of governed agents are changing rapidly.

This is a complementary, not competing, contribution. Safety research asks "how do we keep AI from killing us?" Institutional research asks "how do we keep civilization coherent when AI is changing faster than our institutions can track?"

13.2 Relation to Multi-Agent Governance Literature

The multi-rate governance model connects to existing work on polycentric governance (Ostrom, 2010) and nested institutional hierarchies (Hooghe and Marks, 2003). Our contribution is the explicit introduction of differential capability growth as a variable in governance design, and the formal demonstration that relativistic constraints enforce polycentricity in multiplanetary contexts.

13.3 Relation to Space Colonization Literature

The space colonization literature has traditionally treated AI as a tool for human expansion (Zubrin, 1996; Musk, 2017). More recent work has begun to consider the possibility of autonomous space infrastructure. Our contribution is to make the agent transition problem explicit: what happens when the tools become the agents?

13.4 Relation to Complexity Theory and Verification

Our use of interactive proof systems ($\mathbf{IP} = \mathbf{PSPACE}$) and probabilistically checkable proofs ($\mathbf{PCP}$) to ground asymmetric verification connects to recent work on formal verification of AI systems and machine learning models. However, most of that work focuses on verifying specific properties of AI systems (e.g., robustness, fairness), whereas we focus on verifying governance-relevant claims (e.g., resource usage, safety compliance). The broader framing of verification as a governance primitive is, to our knowledge, novel.

13.5 Relation to Prior Work (C²P)

This paper builds directly on the prior work on the Cognitive Coexistence Principle (C²P), which established the four-dimensional $\Sigma$ framework for substrate-independent intelligence assessment. Where C²P addressed the measurement problem, this paper addresses the governance problem. The two papers form a two-layer architecture:

· Theory layer (C²P): How to measure heterogeneous intelligences on a common scale.
· Applied layer (this work): How to design institutions that remain coherent when measured intelligence is changing at different rates.

The scope boundary is: C²P is concerned with what intelligence is; this paper is concerned with what happens to civilization when different intelligences evolve at different rates.

