Appendix B: Exact and Approximate Safety Constraints

B.1 Deterministic Safety Constraint (Exact)

Feasibility condition:

\tau_d < \tau_{\text{feas}} = \frac{-\ln(1 - \rho_{\max})}{r_A}

Exact bound (when feasible):

k_G \ge \frac{(1 - \rho_{\max}) r_A}{e^{-r_A \tau_d} - (1 - \rho_{\max})}

Derivation:

From $\rho^*_{\text{delay}} \le \rho_{\max}$:

1 - \frac{k_G e^{-r_A \tau_d}}{r_A + k_G} \le \rho_{\max}

\frac{k_G e^{-r_A \tau_d}}{r_A + k_G} \ge 1 - \rho_{\max}

k_G e^{-r_A \tau_d} \ge (1 - \rho_{\max})(r_A + k_G)

k_G e^{-r_A \tau_d} \ge (1 - \rho_{\max}) r_A + (1 - \rho_{\max}) k_G

k_G [e^{-r_A \tau_d} - (1 - \rho_{\max})] \ge (1 - \rho_{\max}) r_A

Assuming feasibility (denominator positive):

k_G \ge \frac{(1 - \rho_{\max}) r_A}{e^{-r_A \tau_d} - (1 - \rho_{\max})}

B.2 Approximate Form (Valid for $\rho_{\max} \ll 1$ and $r_A \tau_d \ll 1$)

Under the small-parameter assumptions, $e^{-r_A \tau_d} \approx 1 - r_A \tau_d$ and $\rho_{\max} \ll 1$ implies $1 - \rho_{\max} \approx 1$. Then:

k_G \ge \frac{r_A}{(1 - r_A \tau_d) - (1 - \rho_{\max})} = \frac{r_A}{\rho_{\max} - r_A \tau_d}

When additionally $r_A \tau_d \ll \rho_{\max}$:

k_G \ge \frac{r_A}{\rho_{\max}} = \frac{1 - \rho_{\max}}{\rho_{\max}} r_A \cdot \frac{1}{1 - \rho_{\max}} \approx \frac{1 - \rho_{\max}}{\rho_{\max}} r_A

Equivalently:

k_G \ge \frac{1 - \rho_{\max}}{\rho_{\max}} r_A e^{r_A \tau_d} \quad \text{(for } r_A \tau_d \ll 1\text{)}

B.3 Stochastic Safety Constraint (Heuristic)

Feasibility condition (heuristic extension):

\tau_d < \frac{-\ln(1 - \rho_{\max})}{r_A + \sigma^2/2}

Volatile tracking constraint:

k_G \ge \frac{(1 - \rho_{\max}) r_A}{e^{-(r_A + \sigma^2/2)\tau_d} - (1 - \rho_{\max})}

Stochastic stability bound (heuristic first-order perturbation):

\tau_d < \tau_c - \frac{\sigma^2}{2k_G^2}

Caveat. The stochastic safety constraint is derived heuristically by replacing $r_A$ with $r_A + \sigma^2/2$ in the deterministic formulas. This reflects the intuition that volatility acts as an additional effective growth rate in the delay exponent (the Itô correction term). A rigorous derivation would require solving the second-moment Lyapunov equation for the delayed stochastic differential equation, which is an open research problem.

