Appendix D: Complexity Theory Background

D.1 Interactive Proof Systems

Definition (Interactive Proof System). A pair of interactive Turing machines $(P, V)$ constitutes an interactive proof system for a language $L$ if:

1. Efficiency: The total communication between $P$ and $V$ is polynomial in the input size.
2. Completeness: If $x \in L$, then $V$ accepts with probability at least $2/3$.
3. Soundness: If $x \notin L$, then for any prover strategy $P^*$, $V$ accepts with probability at most $1/3$.

Theorem (Shamir, 1992). $\mathbf{IP} = \mathbf{PSPACE}$

This means that any language decidable in polynomial space can be verified by a polynomial-time verifier through an interactive protocol with a computationally unbounded prover.

D.2 Probabilistically Checkable Proofs

Definition (PCP). A language $L \in \mathbf{PCP}[r(n), q(n)]$ if there exists a probabilistic polynomial-time verifier $V$ that, given input $x$ and oracle access to a proof $\pi$, uses $r(n)$ random bits and queries at most $q(n)$ bits of $\pi$, such that:

1. Completeness: If $x \in L$, there exists $\pi$ such that $V^\pi(x)$ accepts with probability 1.
2. Soundness: If $x \notin L$, for all $\pi$, $V^\pi(x)$ accepts with probability at most $1/2$.

Theorem (PCP Theorem; Arora et al., 1998). $\mathbf{NP} = \mathbf{PCP}[\mathcal{O}(\log n), \mathcal{O}(1)]$

D.3 Succinct Arguments (SNARKs)

Definition. A succinct non-interactive argument of knowledge (SNARK) for an $\mathbf{NP}$ relation $\mathcal{R}$ consists of three algorithms $(\text{Setup}, \text{Prove}, \text{Verify})$ such that:

· $\text{Setup}(1^\lambda) \to \text{crs}$: Generates a common reference string.
· $\text{Prove}(\text{crs}, x, w) \to \pi$: Generates a proof $\pi$ for statement $x$ given witness $w$.
· $\text{Verify}(\text{crs}, x, \pi) \to \{0, 1\}$: Verifies the proof.

Properties:

· Completeness: If $(x, w) \in \mathcal{R}$, then $\text{Verify}(\text{crs}, x, \text{Prove}(\text{crs}, x, w)) = 1$.
· Soundness: If no witness $w$ exists such that $(x, w) \in \mathcal{R}$, then no polynomial-time adversary can produce a proof $\pi$ that $\text{Verify}$ accepts, except with negligible probability.
· Succinctness: $|\pi| = \text{poly}(\lambda, \log|x|)$ and $\text{Time}(\text{Verify}) = \text{poly}(\lambda, \log|x|)$.

